## The problem

Every `fetch()` you have ever written pointed at something somebody else built. When it returned 400 you read their docs. When it returned 500 you filed a ticket and waited. In standup someone asks whether the profile endpoint could also return the user's follower count, and you cannot answer — not because it is hard, but because you have never opened the file where the answer lives.

Today that file becomes yours. By the end of this module the other side of `fetch()` is thirty lines of TypeScript you wrote, running on your laptop, and you will have called it from a terminal and from a real app. The mystery does not survive this.

## The idea

A server is a program stuck in a loop.

It starts up, asks the operating system for a port, and then blocks: waiting. When a connection arrives it wakes, reads the bytes, decides what to do, writes bytes back, and goes back to waiting. That is all. There is no daemon magic and no special language.

The closest thing in your world is an event listener that never unmounts. `app.get("/notes", handler)` is `addEventListener("GET /notes", handler)` — you register a callback and something else calls it when the event happens. And Node runs on the same kind of single-threaded event loop as the JS thread in your app, which means the same cardinal sin applies: block it and *everything* stops. In RN, a heavy synchronous loop drops frames for one user. On a server, it freezes every request from every user at once.

A **port** is the apartment number at a street address. The IP gets you to the machine; the port gets you to the specific process. Only one process can hold a port at a time, which is what `EADDRINUSE` is telling you.

## In practice

Scaffold it:

```bash
mkdir hello-api && cd hello-api
npm init -y
npm i express
npm i -D typescript tsx @types/express @types/node
npx tsc --init
```

Before Express, see what Express is hiding. This is a complete HTTP server using nothing but Node:

```ts
import { createServer } from "node:http";

const server = createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ ok: true, method: req.method, path: req.url }));
});

server.listen(3000);
```

Run it, curl it, and notice what you do *not* have: no routing (every path hits the same function), no JSON body parsing, no way to compose behaviour. Express is a library that adds those three things on top of exactly this. Knowing that keeps `app.get` from feeling like a framework incantation.

Now the Express version:

```ts
import express from "express";

const app = express();
app.use(express.json()); // turns a JSON request body into req.body

app.get("/health", (_req, res) => {
  res.status(200).json({ status: "ok" });
});

app.get("/greet/:name", (req, res) => {
  const { name } = req.params;             // path param, always a string
  const loud = req.query.loud === "true";  // query param, also always a string
  const message = `hello ${name}`;
  res.json({ message: loud ? message.toUpperCase() : message });
});

app.post("/echo", (req, res) => {
  res.status(201).json({ youSent: req.body });
});

app.listen(3000);
```

Three kinds of input, and it is worth naming which is which. `/greet/:name` puts data in the **path** — use it to identify a resource. `?loud=true` puts data in the **query string** — use it for filters, sorting, pagination. The POST body carries the data you are creating. Getting these three habits right now saves you a redesign in phase 4.

Hit all of it from a terminal:

```bash
curl -i http://localhost:3000/health
curl -i "http://localhost:3000/greet/aswin?loud=true"
curl -i -X POST http://localhost:3000/echo \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"hi":1}'
```

Then run that last one again with the `-H` line deleted. `req.body` comes back `undefined` — not `{}`. That is because `express.json()` only parses a body when the `Content-Type` says JSON, and it is a mystery that has cost people entire afternoons. You now recognise it in ten seconds.

One more sharp edge, visible in the code above: query params are strings.

```ts
const limit = Number(req.query.limit); // "20" becomes 20, absent becomes NaN
```

`req.query.limit` is `"20"`, never `20`, and it is `undefined` when omitted, so `Number()` hands you `NaN` and your slice returns nothing. Tomorrow you will fix this properly with schema coercion instead of hand-written casts.

Finally, call it from a real app:

```ts
const res = await fetch("http://192.168.1.20:3000/greet/aswin?loud=true");
const data = await res.json();
```

Note the LAN IP. From a physical device, `localhost` means the device. From the Android emulator, the host machine is `10.0.2.2`. And recent Android targets block plain HTTP by default, so a cleartext LAN call may need a debug network-security config — that is a known hurdle, not a bug in your server.

## In your world

That file is the shape underneath all five of your practice projects. `GET /tasks?status=open&limit=20` in task-manager-api is this file plus auth, plus validation, plus a database — and nothing conceptually new. The next four days add those layers one at a time on top of what you just typed.

Your work backend swaps Express for Hono and Node for Bun, and the translation is almost mechanical: `app.get("/path", handler)`, a param read off the request context, a JSON response returned rather than written. The three questions a handler answers never change: which method and path is this, what did the caller send, what do I send back. Learning Express first means Hono arrives as new vocabulary rather than a new idea.

## Watch out

- Send exactly one response per handler, then return. Two `res.json()` calls give you "Cannot set headers after they are sent", and it is usually a missing `return` on an early exit.
- Route order matters. Declare `/notes/new` *before* `/notes/:id`, or `:id` swallows the word "new".
- Any synchronous heavy loop blocks every request from every user. Same event-loop rule you know from the JS thread, much higher stakes.
- A forgotten `await` in a handler leaves the client hanging until its timeout, with no error anywhere. Tomorrow's module makes that failure mode explicit.

