## The problem

Three pages, three different nights, one root cause.

A client sends `{ "title": null }` because a text input was cleared and your app spread an undefined into the payload. Your handler calls `title.trim()`. TypeError, 500, alert fires. The user sees "something went wrong" and cannot create a task, and the truth — his request was invalid — was known at the very first line of your code and nobody said it out loud.

A deploy goes out to a new environment with `DATABASE_URL` missing. The process boots. `/health` returns 200. The container is marked healthy and the deploy is green. Twenty minutes later, after you closed your laptop, the first request that touches the database 500s, and it keeps 500ing until someone wakes up.

And when you finally get on the machine to investigate, the logs are four thousand lines of `console.log("here", data)` with no timestamps, no request ids, no severity, interleaved from three processes, and no way to search for the one request the user complained about.

## The idea

One habit, three applications: **validate at the boundary, then trust your own code.**

Everything from outside the process is `unknown` until proven otherwise — request bodies, query strings, headers, environment variables, webhook payloads, third-party responses. Draw a wall at the edge, check things once as they cross, and inside the wall your types are actually true.

The analogy is the door of a venue. ID is checked once, at the entrance, by someone whose whole job is checking. No bartender inside re-checks. If you let unverified people in, every bartender has to be suspicious of everyone forever, and their suspicion will never be as thorough as the door.

Skip the wall and you get defensive code everywhere: `if (!title) return`, `title?.trim() ?? ""`, `Number(limit) || 20`. Each one is an apology for not having checked, and collectively they still miss the case that pages you.

## In practice

**Zod for request bodies.** One schema, and the type comes free:

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

export const createNoteSchema = z.object({
  title: z.string().min(1).max(200),
  body: z.string().max(10_000).default(""),
  tags: z.array(z.string().min(1)).max(10).optional(),
});

export type CreateNoteInput = z.infer<
  typeof createNoteSchema
>;
```

`z.infer` is the point of the whole exercise. The runtime check and the compile-time type are the same declaration, so they cannot drift apart. Two parallel definitions always drift; one definition cannot.

A middleware that applies any schema:

```ts
import type { RequestHandler } from "express";
import type { ZodType, ZodTypeDef } from "zod";

export const validateBody =
  <TBody,>(schema: ZodType<TBody, ZodTypeDef, unknown>): RequestHandler =>
  (req, res, next) => {
    const parsed = schema.safeParse(req.body);
    if (!parsed.success) {
      return res.status(400).json({
        error: "validation_failed",
        issues: parsed.error.issues.map((issue) => ({
          path: issue.path.join("."),
          message: issue.message,
        })),
      });
    }
    req.body = parsed.data; // typed, coerced, defaults applied
    next();
  };
```

Three decisions in there worth defending. **`safeParse`, not `parse`** — a bad body is a 400, and `parse` throws, which without special handling becomes a 500 and blames you for the caller's mistake. **Return the issues** — future-you, debugging the app against this API, needs to know *which* field. **Map the issues yourself** rather than dumping `parsed.error` — the raw error is a library's internal shape, and shipping it makes your public contract depend on your zod version.

Query strings need coercion, because everything arriving in a URL is a string:

```ts
export const listNotesQuery = z.object({
  limit: z.coerce.number().int().min(1).max(100).default(20),
  cursor: z.string().optional(),
  includeDone: z.coerce.boolean().default(false),
});
```

That is the real fix for yesterday's `Number(req.query.limit)` returning `NaN`. The `.max(100)` also quietly closes a denial-of-service door: without it, someone requests a million rows.

**Environment validation at startup.** This file runs before anything else:

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const envSchema = z.object({
  NODE_ENV: z.enum(["development", "test", "production"]).default("development"),
  PORT: z.coerce.number().int().default(3000),
  DATABASE_URL: z.string().url(),
  JWT_SECRET: z.string().min(32),
  LOG_LEVEL: z.enum(["debug", "info", "warn", "error"]).default("info"),
});

const parsed = envSchema.safeParse(process.env);
if (!parsed.success) {
  process.stderr.write(
    `invalid environment:\n${JSON.stringify(parsed.error.flatten().fieldErrors, null, 2)}\n`,
  );
  process.exit(1);
}

export const env = parsed.data;
```

Import `env` everywhere. Never touch `process.env` again outside this file. Two payoffs: `process.env.PORT` is `string | undefined` at every use site, while `env.PORT` is a `number`, once. And crashing on boot is a *feature*. A deploy that dies immediately and loudly gets rolled back in two minutes by a pipeline. A deploy that dies on request four hundred pages a human.

**Structured logging with pino.**

```ts
import pino from "pino";
import { env } from "./env";

export const logger = pino({
  level: env.LOG_LEVEL,
  redact: ["req.headers.authorization", "*.password", "*.token"],
  ...(env.NODE_ENV === "development" ? { transport: { target: "pino-pretty" } } : {}),
});

logger.info({ userId, noteId }, "note_created");
```

Object first, message second. Levels mean something: `debug` is local noise, `info` is a business event worth counting, `warn` is something recovered from, `error` needs a human. Setting `LOG_LEVEL=info` in production then silences debug without touching code.

Why not `console.log`? Three reasons, in order of how much they will cost you. It has no levels, so you cannot turn it down. It emits prose, and prose cannot be filtered, aggregated, or alerted on — `{"level":50,"msg":"note_create_failed","noteId":"a1"}` is a query, `here undefined` is not. And when stdout is a pipe or a file, as it is in every container, `console.log` writes **synchronously** and blocks the event loop, so under load your logging becomes your bottleneck.

Last piece, and the one that makes logs usable: give every request an id.

```ts
import { randomUUID } from "node:crypto";

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const requestId = req.header("x-request-id") ?? randomUUID();
  req.log = logger.child({ requestId });
  res.setHeader("x-request-id", requestId);
  next();
});
```

Use `req.log` inside handlers instead of the root logger and every line from one request shares an id, across every service it touches. Echo that header into your app's error toast and a user's screenshot becomes a database query.

## In your world

All five practice projects begin with the same three files: `env.ts`, `logger.ts`, `validate.ts`. Write them once today, well, and you are copying a known-good foundation four more times instead of relearning it.

Your work backend does the same three things in its own idiom. Route schemas validate the request *and* generate the API description your mobile app is built against, so a schema there is simultaneously the check and the documentation — one more reason the schema is the single source of truth rather than a duplicate of the type. Env validation matters even more on that team than on your practice projects, because merging to main *is* the production deploy: a missing variable caught at boot fails the deploy, and the same variable caught at request time is an incident with users in it.

## Watch out

- `schema.parse()` inside a route throws, and an unhandled ZodError becomes a 500 for what is really a 400. Either use `safeParse` at the boundary or teach your error handler about ZodError. Pick one; do not do both halfway.
- Validating the body and ignoring params, query, and headers. All four are equally attacker-controlled.
- `.optional()`, `.nullable()`, and `.default()` are three different contracts. Your app will feel the difference between a missing key, a null, and a zero.
- Logging whole request bodies from auth routes puts passwords and tokens in your log store forever, including in whatever third-party service ships them. Redact by key, at the logger, not at each call site.

