Build the wrong version first. You need to feel this once so that "just delete the row" stops sounding simpler.

**Set it up.** Add a second store with comments that reference notes:

```ts
// src/comments/comments.store.ts
export interface Comment {
  id: string;
  noteId: string;
  text: string;
}

let comments: readonly Comment[] = [];

export function insert(comment: Comment): Comment {
  comments = [...comments, comment];
  return comment;
}

export function findByNoteId(noteId: string): readonly Comment[] {
  return comments.filter((comment) => comment.noteId === noteId);
}
```

Add a hard-delete endpoint that really removes the note, plus `GET /notes/:id/comments`.

```ts
// in notes.store.ts — the version you will delete afterwards
export function hardDelete(id: string): void {
  notes = notes.filter((note) => note.id !== id);
}
```

**Run the sequence.**

1. `POST /notes` with title "Q3 pricing agreed with vendor".
2. Add two comments on it.
3. `DELETE /notes/:id` using the hard-delete route.
4. `GET /notes/:id` — 404, as expected. Fine so far.
5. `GET /notes/:id/comments` — two comments come back, pointing at a note that does not exist. Your app would render two comment bubbles under a missing parent. Nothing errored. Nothing logged.
6. Now try to answer the question a real user will ask you: *"What did that note say?"* You cannot. Not from the API, not from the logs, not from anywhere. The sentence is gone.
7. And the follow-up your manager will ask: *"Who deleted it, and when?"* Also gone.

**Then flip it.** Delete `hardDelete`, use the soft-delete service from the lesson, and repeat the sequence. `GET /notes/:id` still returns 404 — the resource is still gone from the caller's point of view. But the row is right there with a `deletedAt` timestamp, so both questions have answers, the comments still resolve to real content, and restoring is one update.

**Write down the two lessons.** First: hard delete destroys the answer to questions you have not been asked yet. Second: a real database would have had an opinion here — a foreign key would either have *refused* the delete or cascaded and destroyed the comments too, and both of those are worse surprises when you discover them in production. You will meet foreign keys and `ON DELETE` behaviour on day 7. Arrive already knowing why they matter.

